CAT = "sgsnbQuality"; SEG = "QUA"
Q = [
("A ward audit shows many patients' falls happen at night near the toilet. Which step starts a quality improvement cycle?",
 'Plan a small test of change', ['Ask the night staff to be more careful', 'Wait for the next accreditation visit', 'Remove the audit results from the report'],
 'Quality improvement uses a plan, do, study, act cycle: a change is planned from the data, tried on a small scale, measured and then adopted, adapted or dropped.'),
('Which statement describes holistic care?',
 'Care of body, mind and spirit', ['Care for the main diagnosis only', 'Care that follows the order set only', 'Care that is the same for all patients'],
 'Holistic care looks at the whole person, including physical, psychological, social, cultural and spiritual needs, not only the disease.'),
('A patient newly diagnosed with type 2 diabetes asks how to start. What should the nurse do first when teaching?',
 'Assess what the patient already knows', ['Give a printed leaflet and leave', 'Explain all complications in one session', 'Teach the family instead of the patient'],
 'Effective client education starts with assessing existing knowledge, readiness and learning needs, so teaching is matched to the person and not overloaded.'),
('Which teaching method best confirms a patient has learned to use an inhaler?',
 'Ask for a return demonstration', ['Ask whether he understood', 'Repeat the instructions louder', 'Give him a longer leaflet'],
 'A return demonstration shows the nurse whether a skill is performed correctly; a yes answer does not prove understanding.'),
('A nurse promoting health in a community talks about preventing hypertension. Which is primary prevention?',
 'Encouraging regular exercise and less salt', ["Treating a patient's stroke in hospital", 'Screening adults for kidney damage', 'Rehabilitating after heart surgery'],
 'Primary prevention stops disease before it starts through lifestyle and risk-factor changes; screening is secondary and treatment or rehabilitation is tertiary prevention.'),
('A patient who speaks little English nods when asked if he understands the consent form. What is the best action?',
 'Use a qualified interpreter to check', ['Accept the nod as informed consent', 'Ask his son to sign in his place', 'Skip the explanation to save time'],
 'A nod does not show understanding. A qualified interpreter lets the nurse confirm the person understands the information given before consent is accepted.'),
('Which communication approach helps an anxious patient most?',
 'Open-ended questions with a calm tone', ['Rapid yes or no questions to finish quickly', 'Reassurance that all will be fine', 'Technical terms to show competence'],
 'Open-ended questions and a calm manner invite the patient to express concerns; premature reassurance and jargon block communication.'),
('A patient says, "I feel like nobody cares what happens to me." Which reply is most therapeutic?',
 '"Can you tell me more about that?"', ['"Of course people care about you."', '"You should not think that way."', '"Everyone feels that sometimes."'],
 'An open invitation to explore the feeling shows concern and encourages the patient to talk, whereas denying or generalising the feeling closes the conversation.'),
('A nurse finds a charting error she made earlier. How should she correct it?',
 'Strike one line, initial and date', ['Use correction fluid over the entry', 'Tear out and rewrite the whole page', 'Leave it unchanged to avoid alteration'],
 'A single line leaves the original entry legible, with a dated and initialled correction. Erasing or removing entries makes the record unreliable.'),
('Which of these is an example of critical thinking in nursing?',
 'Linking findings with his trends', ['Following every routine without question', "Repeating the last shift's care plan", 'Waiting for the doctor to interpret findings'],
 'Critical thinking means analysing assessment data, comparing with trends and evidence, and deciding on priorities and actions.'),
('A patient has a fall risk, wet floor reports and a new sedative. How should the nurse set priorities?',
 'Deal with the biggest threat first', ['Do the tasks in the order they were received', 'Treat all risks as equally urgent', 'Leave all the decisions to the next shift'],
 'Priority setting weighs urgency and potential harm; the immediate threats to safety are dealt with first, then the other risks are planned.'),
('In a handover, which approach gives the receiving nurse the most useful information?',
 'A structured summary like SBAR', ['A chronological account of the whole stay', "Only the patient's name and bed number", 'A list of tasks yet to be done'],
 'A structured tool such as SBAR (situation, background, assessment, recommendation) gives concise, complete and consistent information.'),
('What is the main purpose of an incident report after a medication error?',
 'To learn and improve system safety', ['To decide whom to punish', 'To replace charting the event', "To inform only the patient's family"],
 'Incident reporting supports a just culture: the aim is to understand how the error happened and to improve systems, while the event is still documented in the clinical record.'),
('A patient with limited health literacy is to go home on five medicines. Which strategy suits best?',
 'Use plain words and a simple dosing chart', ['Give the pharmacy leaflets only, with no talk', 'Explain once, rapidly, at discharge', 'List the drugs by their chemical names'],
 'Plain language, short steps and a visual chart improve understanding and adherence for people with low health literacy; teach-back confirms learning.'),
('The teach-back method involves which action?',
 'The patient restates it in his own words', ['The nurse repeats the teaching twice', 'The nurse grades the patient with a quiz', 'The patient reads a printed sheet silently'],
 'In teach-back the patient restates the information so the nurse can check, and if needed re-explain, how well the message was understood.'),
('A nurse practises evidence-based care. Which source is most reliable for a change in wound care?',
 'A systematic review of research', ["A senior colleague's long habit", "A product company's brochure", 'A popular health blog'],
 'Systematic reviews and clinical guidelines combine the best available research; opinion, habit and promotional material are weaker evidence.'),
("A patient refuses a bath, saying he is tired. The nurse's best response is to:",
 'Offer to delay it and explore why', ['Insist because the plan says bath', 'Document refusal and never ask again', 'Ask the nursing aide to force it'],
 'Person-centred care respects preferences, explores reasons for refusal and negotiates a time that suits, rather than imposing routine.'),
('What does a person-centred care plan always include?',
 "The patient's own goals and preferences", ["Only the doctor's diagnosis and orders", "Only the ward's usual routine", 'Only what the family wishes for him'],
 'A person-centred plan is built with the patient and reflects what matters to him, along with clinical needs.'),
("A nurse notes that a patient's pain remains 7/10 after the analgesic and the plan. Which reflects holistic reassessment?",
 'Review causes and psychosocial factors', ['Repeat the same dose again at once', 'Tell the patient pain is expected', 'Increase the dose without assessment'],
 'Pain has physical, emotional and cultural dimensions; reassessment looks at cause, effect of treatment and other factors before escalating or adjusting treatment.'),
('Which phrase describes the right use of a patient satisfaction survey in quality improvement?',
 'Feedback to identify areas needing change', ['A way to rank individual nurses publicly', 'A replacement for clinical outcome data', 'A one-time task with no follow-up at all'],
 'Patient feedback is one input among outcome and process data to locate improvement opportunities, with results acted on and fed back.'),
]
